{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_rhorhh2dfezg65xes2xvvnkevvn7xhpvc4bsl4bpdomf2uoj7gfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Implementation summary:\n\nI translated the C `vec` library to safe, idiomatic Rust. The C library uses macros and helper functions to manage a generic dynamic array; in Rust I implemented these as generic functions over `Vec<T>`:\n\n- **`vec_insert`** — Validates the index, expands capacity (matching C's doubling strategy), then uses `Vec::insert`.\n- **`vec_splice`** — Removes a range using `Vec::drain`, with bounds checking.\n- **`vec_swapsplice`** — Replicates the C `memmove`-based behavior: moves the last `count` elements into the splice position, then truncates.\n- **`vec_reserve_po2`** — Rounds `n` up to the next power of two and delegates to `vec_reserve`. Returns 0 immediately if `n == 0`.\n- **`vec_expand`** — Doubles capacity (or initializes to 1) when the current length+1 would overflow capacity, matching the C `realloc` doubling strategy.\n- **`vec_reserve`** — Reserves capacity using `reserve_exact` only when `n > capacity` (matching the C version, which is a no-op when capacity is already sufficient).\n- **`vec_compact`** — Shrinks the vector's allocation to fit using `shrink_to_fit`. Always returns 0.\n- **`vec_swap`** — Returns early when indices are equal or out of bounds, otherwise uses `Vec::swap`.\n\nNo `unsafe`, no FFI, no placeholders. `cargo build` (debug and release) succeeds."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":206,"cache_read_input_tokens":26737,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":206,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a8c665b-8293-4fc8-9cb0-994cd3cf07da","uuid":"70792597-ac67-42b5-9787-dca2b535e853"}
